/*!
\title Interactive Shapes
\ingroup quickgraphlibdemos
\brief Demonstrates selectable, draggable, and resizable shapes.
\example interactiveshapes

\image examples/InteractiveShapes.svg

This example demonstrates how to create interactive shapes that can be selected,
moved, and resized using mouse interactions.

\section1 Interactive Features

The rectangle in this example supports:

\list
\li \b{Selection}: Click the rectangle to select it (highlights with blue border)
\li \b{Deselection}: Click outside the rectangle to deselect it
\li \b{Moving}: Drag the selected rectangle to move it around the graph
\li \b{Resizing}: Drag the corner handles to resize the rectangle
\endlist

\section2 Implementation Details

The interactive rectangle uses several QML components:

\list
\li \b{ShapePath}: Renders the rectangle with data coordinates
\li \b{MouseArea}: Handles selection and dragging within the rectangle bounds
\li \b{Repeater with handles}: Creates resize handles at each corner when selected
\li \b{Coordinate transforms}: Uses \c{dataTransform.map()} and \c{dataTransform.inverted().map()}
    to convert between pixel and data coordinates
\endlist

\section2 Coordinate Transformation

The key to making shapes interactive is properly transforming between:
\list
\li \b{Data coordinates}: The logical coordinate system of your graph
\li \b{Pixel coordinates}: The screen position where mouse events occur
\endlist

Use \c{axes.dataTransform.map()} to convert from data to pixel coordinates,
and \c{axes.dataTransform.inverted().map()} to convert from pixel to data coordinates.

\quotefile examples/InteractiveShapes.qml
*/
